\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The Hurwitz basis as matrices modulo three}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The basis #{(1,\qi,\qj,h)} of \ref{fdip-00V1} has
images in #{M_2(\mathbb F_3)}
##{\begin{aligned}
 Q_1&=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},&
 Q_i&=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\2&0\end{pmatrix},\\
 Q_j&=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},&
 Q_h&=\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\1&1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
They define an algebra isomorphism
##{\mathcal O/3\mathcal O\ \cong\ M_2(\mathbb F_3).}
Quaternion conjugation becomes the matrix adjugate, and norm
becomes determinant. Left multiplication on columns gives an
irreducible module #{S=\mathbb F_3^2} for #{2T}, with
commutant #{\mathbb F_3}.}

\proof{
\p{Direct multiplication gives
#{Q_i^2=Q_j^2=-Q_1}, #{Q_iQ_j=-Q_jQ_i}, and
#{Q_h=(Q_1+Q_i+Q_j+Q_iQ_j)/2} over #{\mathbb F_3}.
Since #{2} is invertible here, these relations reproduce
the quaternion products and the Hurwitz multiplication table
of \ref{fdip-00V1}. They define the algebra map.}

\p{Flattening each matrix in row order gives the rows of
##{\begin{pmatrix}
1&0&0&1\\0&1&2&0\\1&1&1&2\\0&2&1&1
\end{pmatrix}.}
Subtract its first row from the third. The remaining
#{3\times 3} determinant is
#{0-2(1-2)=2} modulo #{3}. Thus the 4 images form a
basis, proving bijectivity. This gives an explicit basis
version of the algebra identification in \ref{fdip-00MK};
its #{h} is the element #{\omega+1}.}

\p{For two-by-two matrices the adjugate is linear.
It fixes #{Q_1}, negates #{Q_i,Q_j}, and sends #{Q_h}
to #{Q_1-Q_h}, just as quaternion conjugation acts on the
basis. Hence conjugation becomes adjugation on every element.
The identity #{X\adjugate X=(\det X)Q_1} then identifies
the norm with the determinant. In particular all unit images
lie in #{\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb F_3)}.}

\p{The unit images span the full matrix algebra, because
the 4 basis elements are units. A nonzero subspace of
#{S} invariant under them is invariant under every matrix.
A matrix can send any chosen nonzero vector to any target
vector, so this subspace is all of #{S}. An endomorphism
commuting with all matrices is scalar: commuting with the
two diagonal matrix units makes it diagonal, and commuting
with an off-diagonal matrix unit equates its diagonal entries.
This proves the two module assertions.}
}
